<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Néstor Kirchner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a>–<a href="/years/2010">2010</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Néstor Kirchner was an Argentine Peronist politician, governor of Santa Cruz province, who served as president from 2003 to 2007 after the crisis of 2001. His government restructured the defaulted debt, paid off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and presided over rapid growth driven by commodity exports, and it reopened trials for the crimes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cristina-fernandez-de-kirchner">Cristina Fernández de Kirchner</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nestor-kirchner.jpg" alt="Néstor Kirchner" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Casa Rosada - Gobierno de la Nación · CC BY 2.5 ar · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presidente_Nestor_Kirchner_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Néstor Kirchner was born on 25 February 1950 in Río Gallegos, then a federal territory. His mother, María Juana Ostoich Dragnic, was Chilean and of Croatian descent. He studied law at the National University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-plata">La Plata</a>, where he met Cristina Fernández, and the couple returned to Río Gallegos to open a law firm. He ran for mayor of Río Gallegos in 1987 and for governor of Santa Cruz in 1991. Although he received only 30 percent of the vote, below the 36 percent of the UCR, the Ley de Lemas added the votes of another Peronist faction to his own and he was elected. He was reelected in 1995 and 1999 after an amendment to the provincial constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Eduardo Duhalde, appointed president by Congress after the December 2001 riots, urged Kirchner to run in 2003 to prevent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a>'s return. Menem won a plurality in the first round but resigned, fearing defeat in the runoff, and Kirchner took office on 25 May 2003. He kept Roberto Lavagna as minister of economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> negotiated a swap of its defaulted debt and repaid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a>. Several Supreme Court judges, among them its president Julio Nazareno, resigned while facing impeachment, and new judges were appointed.</p><p>Kirchner regarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirty-war">Dirty War</a> as an unresolved issue. Congress annulled the full stop and due obedience laws in August 2003, and in 2005 the Supreme Court declared them and Menem's presidential pardons unconstitutional, so that many cases against the military were reopened. In foreign policy he opposed the proposed Free Trade Area of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and sent forces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. In the 2005 midterm elections his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cristina-fernandez-de-kirchner">Cristina Fernández de Kirchner</a> defeated Hilda González de Duhalde in the election for senator for Buenos Aires Province.</p><p>Kirchner did not seek reelection in 2007, and his wife ran and was elected. He stayed influential, leading the PJ, and in December 2007 took part in Operation Emmanuel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, which sought the release of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutionary-armed-forces-of-colombia">FARC</a> hostages. He was narrowly defeated in the 2009 midterm election for deputy of Buenos Aires Province and was appointed Secretary General of UNASUR in 2010.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kirchner died of cardiac arrest on 27 October 2010 at the age of 60, at home in El Calafate. He had undergone surgery on a carotid artery in February and an angioplasty in September. His body was flown to the Casa Rosada for a state funeral, and three national days of mourning were declared.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Néstor Kirchner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Néstor Kirchner</b> and Cristina Fernández de Kirchner presided over a commodity-led recovery from 2003 to 2015, followed by the centre-right Mauricio Macri and the Peronist Alberto Fernández.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The words have stayed the same, but groups within Peronism have added their own verses, among them the Montoneros and Guardia de Hierro, and in recent years verses about the governments of <b>Néstor Kirchner</b> and Cristina Fernández de Kirchner.</blockquote><a href="/songs/peronist-march">Peronist March</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Río Gallegos, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in El Calafate, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-51.62,-69.22&amp;year=1989" aria-label="Río Gallegos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Néstor Kirchner"><image href="/maps/globes/g300s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4513 6825h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4513 6825h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-51.62,-69.22&amp;year=1989">What was here in 1989? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
